/*  A simplified command shell, read-eval-loop over standard input.

    Anything on your PATH is launched, with as many arguments as you type. `Leave.` on a line by
    itself exits; so does end of input. A trailing `&` runs a command in the background.

    The built-ins are `cd`, `jobs`, `fg` and `bg` — built in for the same reason every shell builds
    them in: a child process cannot change its parent's directory, and it cannot reach its parent's
    job table either.

    Arrows, Home/End, Ctrl-U and a history of what you typed all come from `tools/terminal.cufe`,
    which the REPL uses too. Not one line of editing is written here — that is the whole reason
    the terminal is a book and not a part of the REPL.

    Three things worth noticing:

    - There is no shell underneath this shell. `run` is fork + execvp directly —
        the program name and each argument cross to the child as separate OS-level
        arguments, so nothing is ever re-parsed by /bin/sh and quoting cannot bite.

    - Launching is the shell's OWN, not Cufet's `Run`, and that is the one place this drops out of
        the language into C. A shell needs each job in its own process group and the terminal handed
        to whichever group is in the foreground; `Run` deliberately does neither. Without that there
        is no Ctrl-Z, no `bg`, and no `fg` — a job could be started and never parked.

        ⚠ This is not a gap in Cufet. `run … with the terminal` is right for a program that opens an
        editor and reads its exit code; a shell is the special case underneath it. Correct layering,
        and the axiom block below is where it happens.

    - The arguments are a SERIES, not a written-out list. A shell cannot know how many arguments
        it will be handed until it has read the line, so a list fixed when the shell was compiled
        could only ever be a guess with a ceiling on it — and this one had a ceiling of three.

    There is no list of permitted commands. A program that does not exist is a launch failure and
    says so, which is the same answer a whitelist gave and one fewer thing to keep current.

    Run it interpreted, or compile it — `cufet build tools/shell.cufe` produces
    a native binary with no managed runtime under it. The subprocess machinery is
    POSIX, so a compiled shell needs Linux, macOS, or WSL. */


/*  Does a name fit a pattern? `*` stands for any run of characters including none, `?` for
    exactly one.

    ★ Recursive rather than a loop, because `*` is the only hard case and recursion states it
    exactly: either the star matches nothing and the rest of the pattern must fit here, or it
    swallows one more character and the same question is asked again. A loop over this needs a
    backtracking index that says the same thing less clearly. */
Bind fact to matches, given (the text pattern, the text name):
    If the length of pattern is 0, return the length of name is 0.

    Define head as the characters from 1 to 1 of pattern.
    Define rest-pattern as the characters from 2 to the end of pattern.

    If head is "*":
        If cast matches on (rest-pattern, name), return true.
        If the length of name is 0, return false.
        Define shorter as the characters from 2 to the end of name.
        Return cast matches on (pattern, shorter).
    Done.

    If the length of name is 0, return false.
    Define first as the characters from 1 to 1 of name.
    Define rest-name as the characters from 2 to the end of name.

    If head is "?", return cast matches on (rest-pattern, rest-name).
    If head is first, return cast matches on (rest-pattern, rest-name).
    Return false.
Done.

/* Where the last path separator sits, or 0 for a bare name. Both separators count, so a pattern
   typed the Windows way finds its directory too. */
Bind number to last-separator, given (the text word):
    Define found as 0.
    For each at in range 1 to the length of word, repeat:
        Define here as the characters from at to at of word.
        If here is "/", found becomes at.
        If here is "\\", found becomes at.
    Done.
    Return found.
Done.

/* Whether a word is a pattern at all. A word with no `*` and no `?` is never looked up. */
Bind fact to wild, given (the text word):
    For each at in range 1 to the length of word, repeat:
        Define here as the characters from at to at of word.
        If here is "*", return true.
        If here is "?", return true.
    Done.
    Return false.
Done.

/*  ⚠ A listing hands back JOINED paths — `tools/shell.cufe`, not `shell.cufe` — so the name has to
    be cut back out of one before it can be matched against a pattern. */
Bind text to base-name, given (the text whole):
    Define cut as cast last-separator on (whole).
    If cut is 0, return whole.
    Define from-here as cut + 1.
    Return the characters from from-here to the end of whole.
Done.

/*  One word, expanded into the names it stands for.

    ★★ A word that matches NOTHING stands as itself. That looks like giving up and is the rule every
    shell follows: a pattern meant for the program rather than for the shell has to survive the trip,
    and a typo is better reported by whatever was asked for the file than guessed at here.

    ★ The directory part is kept exactly as it was typed and put back in front of each match, so
    a pattern with a directory written in front of it answers names carrying that same directory,
    while a bare `*.cufe` answers `shell.cufe`. Reading the
    current directory to answer the bare case would spell every name `./shell.cufe` — the same file,
    and not what anyone typed.

    ⚠ A leading dot is matched only when it was asked for. `*` skipping hidden names is not an
    accident of the matcher; it is the one place the pattern language deliberately does not mean
    what it says, and every shell agrees to it. */
Bind series of text to expanded, given (the text word):
    Define answer as a series of text.

    If cast wild on (word) is false:
        Insert word into answer.
        Return answer.
    Done.

    Define cut as cast last-separator on (word).
    Define here as ".".
    Define carrying as "".
    Define pattern as word.
    If cut is greater than 0:
        Define up-to as cut - 1.
        The here becomes the characters from 1 to up-to of word.
        /* A leading separator: the directory IS the separator, and cutting it away would look here
           instead of at the root. */
        If the length of here is 0, here becomes the characters from cut to cut of word.
        The carrying becomes the characters from 1 to cut of word.
        Define from-here as cut + 1.
        The pattern becomes the characters from from-here to the end of word.
    Done.

    Try to:
        Define listed as the contents of the directory here.
        /* Sorted, so one pattern gives one order twice over. The listing does not promise an order,
           and a command line that shuffles between runs is its own kind of bug. */
        Define ordered as listed sorted.
        For each whole in ordered, repeat:
            Define name as cast base-name on (whole).
            Define hidden as (the characters from 1 to 1 of name) is ".".
            Define asked as (the characters from 1 to 1 of pattern) is ".".
            If hidden is false or asked:
                If cast matches on (pattern, name), insert "{carrying}{name}" into answer.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        /* A missing or unreadable directory expands to nothing, and says nothing: the word then
           stands, and whatever was asked for it reports what is actually wrong. */
        Define unreadable as true.
    Done.

    If the number of answer is 0, insert word into answer.
    Return answer.
Done.


/*  Job control needs what a shell needs and a program does not: a child in its OWN process group,
    and the terminal handed to whichever group is in the foreground. Cufet's own `Run` deliberately
    does neither — it launches, waits, and hands the terminal over wholesale — so a shell that wants
    to suspend and resume has to own the machinery itself. That is what this block is.

    ⚠ This is the ONE place the shell drops out of Cufet, and it is on purpose. `run … with the
    terminal` remains right for a program that wants to open an editor and read its exit code; a
    shell is the special case that needs setpgid and tcsetpgrp underneath. Correct layering rather
    than a gap in the language.

    ★ A series cannot cross into an axiom — only a number, a fact, a voidable number, a voidable
    address and a voidable text do. So argv is BUILT on the C side through a held address, one word
    at a time. The address lives in a rabbit and is freed when that rabbit ends, which is why every
    launch below opens one. */
Pull a book on the c-language.

    Define c-language number words-free, given (the address vec), as [({
        char** v = (char**)the vec;
        if (v) { for (int i = 0; v[i]; i++) free(v[i]); free(v); }
        0;
    })].

    /*  64 slots and a NULL terminator's worth of room. A command line longer than that is not a
        command line, and refusing at a ceiling beats scribbling past the end of the vector. */
    Define c-language voidable address words-new as [(void*)calloc(64, sizeof(char*))],
        and free it with words-free.

    /*  ⚠ strdup, because the bytes belong to Cufet and the child is exec'd after this returns.
        Pointing argv at memory Cufet may reuse is the kind of bug that works until it does not. */
    Define c-language number words-add, given (the address vec, the text word), as [({
        char** v = (char**)the vec;
        int at = 0;
        while (at < 62 && v[at]) at++;
        v[at] = strdup(the word);
        at + 1;
    })].

    /*  ⚠⚠ SIGTTOU IGNORED, and the shell stops itself without it. Handing the terminal to a job and
        taking it back are both writes to the terminal's foreground group, and a process that is not
        already in the foreground gets SIGTTOU for doing that — which stops the shell, at the exact
        moment it was trying to take control back. Ignoring it is what every shell does and why. */
    Define c-language number ignore-terminal-stops as [({
        signal(SIGTTOU, SIG_IGN);
        signal(SIGTTIN, SIG_IGN);
        0;
    })].

    /*  Launch, in the job-control shape: the child gets a process group of its own, named after its
        own pid, and the terminal is handed to that group when the job is to run in the foreground.

        ★ setpgid is called in BOTH parent and child, deliberately. Whichever runs first wins and the
        other is a harmless no-op — and doing it in only one leaves a window where the shell has
        already tried to give the terminal to a group that does not exist yet. */
    Define c-language number launch, given (the address vec, the fact foreground), as [({
        char** v = (char**)the vec;
        pid_t kid = (v && v[0]) ? fork() : (pid_t)-1;
        if (kid == 0) {
            setpgid(0, 0);
            if (the foreground) tcsetpgrp(0, getpid());
            /*  The child inherits the shell's ignored signals otherwise, and a program that cannot
                be stopped or interrupted is not what anyone typed. */
            signal(SIGTTOU, SIG_DFL);
            signal(SIGTTIN, SIG_DFL);
            signal(SIGINT,  SIG_DFL);
            signal(SIGQUIT, SIG_DFL);
            signal(SIGTSTP, SIG_DFL);
            execvp(v[0], v);
            _exit(127);
        }
        if (kid > 0) {
            setpgid(kid, kid);
            if (the foreground) tcsetpgrp(0, kid);
        }
        (int)kid;
    })].

    /*  The terminal comes back to the shell's own group. Called after every foreground job settles,
        however it settled — finished, killed, or stopped. */
    Define c-language number take-terminal-back as [(int)tcsetpgrp(0, getpgrp())].

    Define c-language number give-terminal-to, given (the number pgid),
        as [(int)tcsetpgrp(0, (pid_t)(int)the pgid)].

    /*  Wait for a foreground job to settle. WUNTRACED is what makes Ctrl-Z visible here: without it
        a stopped child looks exactly like one that is still running, and the shell hangs forever
        waiting for a job the user has already parked.

        0 finished · 1 stopped · -1 nothing to wait for */
    Define c-language number wait-for-foreground, given (the number pid), as [({
        int st = 0;
        pid_t got = waitpid((pid_t)(int)the pid, &st, WUNTRACED);
        (int)(got < 0 ? -1 : (WIFSTOPPED(st) ? 1 : 0));
    })].

    /*  ★ Asked about ONE job, because only one number crosses back and the shell already knows which
        pid it asked about. `waitpid(-1, ...)` would need to return the pid as well as the state, and
        encoding two answers into one number to save a loop over a handful of jobs is a worse trade
        than the loop.

        0 still running · 1 finished · 2 stopped · 3 continued · -1 gone */
    Define c-language number poll-job, given (the number pid), as [({
        int st = 0;
        pid_t got = waitpid((pid_t)(int)the pid, &st, WNOHANG | WUNTRACED | WCONTINUED);
        (int)(got == 0 ? 0
              : got < 0 ? -1
              : WIFSTOPPED(st) ? 2
              : WIFCONTINUED(st) ? 3
              : 1);
    })].

    /*  To the GROUP, not the process. A job is everything the command started, and a program that
        forked helpers should stop and resume with them rather than leaving them behind. */
    Define c-language number signal-job, given (the number pgid, the number sig),
        as [(int)kill(-(pid_t)(int)the pgid, (int)the sig)].

    Define c-language number stop-signal     as [SIGTSTP].
    Define c-language number continue-signal as [SIGCONT].

    Cast ignore-terminal-stops.


    Pull a terminal.
        /* Prompts are for somebody typing. Piped or redirected, they would land in the output. */
        Define asking as cast terminal's interactive.

        /* Raw mode is asked for, never assumed. A pipe, a redirect, or a machine without termios all
           say no, and the answer is believed — the reader falls back to letting the terminal collect
           the line, which is what this shell did before it had an editor at all. */
        Define editing as false.
        If asking, editing becomes cast terminal's raw-on.

        /* Everything typed, in order, for the up arrow to walk back through. */
        Define past as a series of text.

        /*  Everything launched and not yet finished. A job is a pid, the line that started it, and
            whether it is running or stopped — and it lives HERE rather than in C, because an address
            cannot outlive its rabbit while this has to outlive every launch.

            ★ The pid doubles as the process-group id, because `launch` puts each child in a group
            named after itself. One number, two jobs, and no second table to keep in step. */
        Define jobs as a series of records like (the number pid, the text name, the number stopped).

        Define running as true.
        While running, repeat:
            /*  ⚠ Polled here, once per prompt, rather than caught with a SIGCHLD handler. A handler
                would mean an axiom installing one alongside Cufet's own signal machinery, for news
                that is only ever read at the prompt anyway — which is where bash effectively
                reports it too.

                ★ Asked job by job. Only one number crosses back from an axiom, and the shell already
                knows which pid it asked about, so `waitpid(-1, ...)` would have to encode two answers
                into one number to save a loop over a handful of jobs. */
            Define surviving as a series of records like (the number pid, the text name, the number stopped).
            For each job in jobs, repeat:
                Define news as cast poll-job on (the pid of job).
                If news is 0, insert job into surviving.
                If news is 2, insert a record with (the pid (the pid of job), the name (the name of job), the stopped 1) into surviving.
                If news is 3, insert a record with (the pid (the pid of job), the name (the name of job), the stopped 0) into surviving.
                If news is 1, state "[done] {the name of job}".
                If news is less than 0, state "[done] {the name of job}".
            Done.
            The jobs becomes surviving.

            If asking, cast terminal's put on ("cufetsh$ ").

            Define raw as cast terminal's typed on (editing, past).

            /* Void means end of input — the user closed the stream instead of saying Leave. */
            If raw is void, the running becomes false.
            If raw is not void:
                Define line as raw trimmed.

                /* Remembered whether or not it worked. Recalling a line to fix it is most of what a
                   history is for, and the line that failed is the one you most want back. */
                If the length of line is greater than 0, insert line into past.

                If line is "Leave.", running becomes false.
                If line is not "Leave.":
                    /* A blank line is not an error; it is just nothing to do. */
                    If the length of line is greater than 0:
                        Define parts as line split by " ".
                        Define cmd   as the first of parts.
                        Define n     as the number of parts.

                        /*  A trailing `&` is not an argument, it is a request. Shortening the count
                            rather than rebuilding the series is what keeps it out of argv: everything
                            below counts up to `n`, so moving `n` back one word removes it everywhere
                            at once.

                            ⚠ Guarded on a second word, because a line that is only `&` has no command
                            to background and would otherwise leave `cmd` holding the ampersand. */
                        Define builtin as false.
                        Define background as false.
                        If n is greater than 1:
                            If (item n of parts) is "&":
                                background becomes true.
                                The n becomes n - 1.
                            Done.
                        Done.

                        /* `cd` is the shell's own, not a program to launch. A child process cannot change
                           its parent's directory, which is why every real shell builds this one in. With
                           it, `pwd` finally reports somewhere you chose. A bad path is a
                           failure value, so a typo costs you a line of output and not the session. */
                        If cmd is "cd":
                            builtin becomes true.
                            Try to:
                                If n is 1:
                                    Define home as the environment variable "HOME" but void is "".
                                    If the length of home is 0, home becomes the environment variable "USERPROFILE" but void is "/".
                                    The current directory becomes home.
                                Done.
                                If n is 2, the current directory becomes item 2 of parts.
                                If n is greater than 2, state "cufetsh: cd: too many arguments".
                            Done.
                            In case of failure:
                                State "cufetsh: cd: {the message of the failure}".
                            Done.
                        Done.

                        /*  ★ `jobs`, `fg` and `bg` are the shell's own for the same reason `cd` is: a
                            child process cannot change its parent's job table any more than it can
                            change its parent's directory. Every real shell builds these in, and this
                            is why.

                            The argument is a POSITION in the list, not a pid — `fg 2` is the second
                            job, which is what is on screen when you typed `jobs`. Bare `fg` and `bg`
                            take the last one, the way they do everywhere. */
                        If cmd is "jobs":
                            builtin becomes true.
                            Define spot as 1.
                            For each job in jobs, repeat:
                                Define how as "running".
                                If (the stopped of job) is 1, how becomes "stopped".
                                State "[{spot}] {how}  {the name of job}".
                                The spot becomes spot + 1.
                            Done.
                            If the number of jobs is 0, state "cufetsh: no jobs".
                        Done.

                        If cmd is "fg":
                            builtin becomes true.
                            /*  ⚠ The terminal FIRST, then SIGCONT. A job woken while the shell still owns the
                                terminal writes into a foreground group that is not its own and takes SIGTTOU for
                                it — stopping again, immediately, for trying to do the thing it was just told to. */
                            /*  ★ Bare only, taking the most recent job. `fg 2` wants a POSITION parsed out of text,
                                which is a fallible read this does not need yet — the list is short and the last one
                                is nearly always the one meant. */
                            If the number of jobs is 0, state "cufetsh: fg: no jobs".
                            If the number of jobs is greater than 0:
                                Define chosen as item (the number of jobs) of jobs.
                                State the name of chosen.
                                Cast give-terminal-to on (the pid of chosen).
                                Cast signal-job on (the pid of chosen, cast continue-signal).

                                Define left as a series of records like (the number pid, the text name, the number stopped).
                                For each other-job in jobs, repeat:
                                    If (the pid of other-job) is not (the pid of chosen), insert other-job into left.
                                Done.

                                Define settled as cast wait-for-foreground on (the pid of chosen).
                                Cast take-terminal-back.
                                If settled is 1:
                                    State "".
                                    State "[stopped] {the name of chosen}".
                                    Insert a record with (the pid (the pid of chosen), the name (the name of chosen), the stopped 1) into left.
                                Done.
                                The jobs becomes left.
                            Done.
                        Done.

                        If cmd is "bg":
                            builtin becomes true.
                            /*  No terminal, and that is the whole difference from `fg`. A background job that is
                                handed the terminal will read from it, and two things reading one keyboard is how
                                a shell loses characters. */
                            /*  ★ Bare only, taking the most recent job. `fg 2` wants a POSITION parsed out of text,
                                which is a fallible read this does not need yet — the list is short and the last one
                                is nearly always the one meant. */
                            If the number of jobs is 0, state "cufetsh: bg: no jobs".
                            If the number of jobs is greater than 0:
                                Define chosen as item (the number of jobs) of jobs.
                                State the name of chosen.
                                Cast signal-job on (the pid of chosen, cast continue-signal).

                                Define left as a series of records like (the number pid, the text name, the number stopped).
                                For each other-job in jobs, repeat:
                                    If (the pid of other-job) is not (the pid of chosen), insert other-job into left.
                                Done.

                                Insert a record with (the pid (the pid of chosen), the name (the name of chosen), the stopped 0) into left.
                                The jobs becomes left.
                            Done.
                        Done.

                        /* Everything after the command word is an argument, however many there are. */
                        If builtin is false:
                            Define argv as a series of text.
                        
                            /*  ⚠ Guarded, because `range 2 to 1` is not empty — a range with its start past its end
                                counts DOWN, so a one-word line would walk indexes 2 and 1 and ask for an argument
                                that is not there. The guard says the real condition: there is a second word. */
                            If n is greater than 1:
                                For each spot in range 2 to n, repeat:
                                    Define standing-for as cast expanded on (item spot of parts).
                                    For each one-name in standing-for, repeat:
                                        Insert one-name into argv.
                                    Done.
                                Done.
                            Done.

                            /*  ⚠ The terminal goes back to normal for the child, and this is not a courtesy.
                                Raw mode is a property of the TERMINAL, not of this process, so a child launched
                                while it is on inherits no echo, no line editing and no Ctrl-C — `cat` would take
                                keys one at a time in silence and could not be interrupted. Handing the terminal
                                over means handing it over as found. */
                            If editing, cast terminal's raw-off.

                            /*  ⚠⚠ NOT `Run`, and every job goes this way — background and foreground
                                alike. A second launch path would mean half the jobs could be suspended
                                and half could not, which is worse than none of them being able to.

                                ★ The rabbit is what owns the argv vector: an address cannot be held
                                outside one, and this is the block it lives and dies in. One per launch.

                                ⚠ A launch failure is the CHILD's news, not the parent's. fork succeeds
                                and execvp fails, so there is nothing for the shell to catch — the child
                                exits 127, the way every shell has reported `command not found` since
                                the beginning. Cufet's own `Run` uses a close-on-exec pipe to turn that
                                back into a failure value; this shell reads the status instead, which is
                                one fewer moving part for the same answer.
                            */
                            Pull a rabbit.
                                Define words as cast words-new.
                                If words is void, state "cufetsh: out of memory".
                                If words is not void:
                                    Cast words-add on (words, cmd).
                                    For each one-word in argv, repeat:
                                        Cast words-add on (words, one-word).
                                    Done.

                                    Define kid as cast launch on (words, background is false).
                                    If kid is less than 1, state "cufetsh: could not start {cmd}".
                                    If kid is greater than 0:
                                        /*  A backgrounded job is remembered and not waited for — that is
                                            the whole of `&`. The prompt comes straight back, and the poll
                                            at the top of the next line is what notices it finishing. */
                                        If background:
                                            Insert a record with (the pid kid, the name line, the stopped 0) into jobs.
                                            State "[{the number of jobs}] {kid}".
                                        Done.

                                        If background is false:
                                            Define settled as cast wait-for-foreground on (kid).

                                            /*  Taken back before anything is printed. The shell writing to
                                                a terminal whose foreground group is still the child's is the
                                                thing SIGTTOU exists to punish. */
                                            Cast take-terminal-back.

                                            /*  ⚠ A stopped job is REMEMBERED, or Ctrl-Z would park a program
                                                where nothing could ever reach it again. This is the line that
                                                makes suspending reversible. */
                                            If settled is 1:
                                                State "".
                                                Insert a record with (the pid kid, the name line, the stopped 1) into jobs.
                                                State "[{the number of jobs}] stopped  {line}".
                                            Done.
                                        Done.
                                    Done.
                                Done.
                            Done.

                            /*  ⚠⚠ DO NOT DELETE THIS LINE BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE IT DOES NOTHING. Mentioning interrupts
                                ANYWHERE in a program is what puts that program in charge of its own — a program that
                                never mentions them is stopped by Ctrl-C, which is right for a script and fatal for a
                                shell. The mention is the opt-in; the poll is what it is spelled with.
                        
                                ★ It is usually a no-op, and that is correct. While a launched child holds the
                                terminal the interrupt is the CHILD’s, so nothing is recorded here for this line to
                                find. It earns its place when a Ctrl-C lands anywhere else — at the prompt with the
                                line editor unavailable, say — where it stops that from ending the session. */
                            If an interrupt is requested:
                                /*  The terminal echoed `^C` where the cursor happened to be, and nothing has ended the
                                    line since — so the next prompt would be drawn onto the end of it. One blank line
                                    puts it back at the left, which is the whole of what bash does here.
                        
                                    ★ It knows because the flag is RECORDED during a child now. Reading the child’s exit
                                    status would be the other way to know, and is a language question with no spelling
                                    yet — this needs none of it. */
                                State "".
                                Acknowledge the interrupt.
                            Done.

                            /* Taken back for the next line. A child that changed the terminal and did not change
                               it back is why this asks again rather than assuming. */
                            If editing, editing becomes cast terminal's raw-on.
                        Done.
                    Done.
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.

        /* However the loop ended — Leave., end of input, or Ctrl-D — the terminal is handed back the
           way a program that never touched it would leave one. */
        If editing, cast terminal's raw-off.
    Done.
Done.
